> What's the easiest way to manage citations on a google doc? I don't actually want to have a citation manager-- I find that I lose a lot of time putting the citations into their format. Instead, I already have the citations formatted, but it would be nice for something to make sure that every (Author Year) reference has an entry in the references list and visa versa.
**assistant**:
> Managing citations manually in Google Docs while ensuring consistency and completeness can be streamlined with a methodical approach, even without a citation manager. Here's a step-by-step guide for managing this process:
> 
> 1. **Create a Consistent Reference Format:**
>    Make sure all your citations are formatted uniformly according to the style you are using (e.g., APA, MLA). This will help you quickly visually verify references.
> 
> 2. **Utilize Google Docs Tools:**
>    - Use the **Find (Ctrl+F)** feature to highlight each (Author Year) citation in your document. You can then quickly verify if each has a corresponding entry in your reference list.
>    - To go from the Reference List back to citations, copy a part of the reference (e.g., author's last name) and use the Find feature again to locate where it's mentioned in the text.
> 
> 3. **Cross-Check List:** 
>    - Create a tabular list (or simply another section) of all (Author Year) in-text citations used throughout the document.
>    - Cross-check this against your reference list. As you confirm each citation is present in both places, you can mark or highlight them to indicate they are matched.
> 
> 4. **Manual Check Points:**
>    - Dedicate time to doing one final thorough read-through of the document, specifically focusing on these citations. It's an opportunity to catch any missing or mismatched citations.
>    
> 5. **Commenting for Verification:**
>    - If you're collaborating, use Google Docs' "Comment" feature to leave notes on citations that seem problematic or need verification. This can serve as a reminder for yourself, or communicate needed checks to collaborators.
> 
> 6. **Automated Solutions:**
>    Even though you're not keen on citation managers, a simple script could be created to automate this, checking for author-year patterns in the text and comparing them to the reference list. However, you'd need minimal programming to set it up.
> 
> While this approach won't automatically check for citation-list matches like a citation manager might, it leverages Google Docs' built-in features to efficiently manage and cross-reference citations manually.
